{
  "concept": "A line's equation y = mx + b can be matched to its graph by reading b as the y-intercept and m as the signed rise-over-run that controls direction and steepness.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "rectangular coordinate grid with light gray unit gridlines",
    "x-axis labeled x with arrowheads and tick labels from -7 to 7",
    "y-axis labeled y with arrowheads and tick labels from -5 to 5",
    "origin label 0",
    "orange line I with arrowheads at both ends, steep positive slope, crossing the y-axis near 3",
    "Roman numeral label I in orange near the top of the orange line",
    "light blue line II with arrowheads at both ends, shallow positive slope, crossing the y-axis at 3 and the x-axis near -6",
    "Roman numeral label II in light blue near the upper right endpoint",
    "teal line III with arrowheads at both ends, steep positive slope, crossing the y-axis at -3 and the x-axis near 1.5",
    "Roman numeral label III in teal near the upper right portion of the line",
    "dark blue line IV with arrowheads at both ends, steep negative slope, crossing the y-axis at 3 and descending to the lower right",
    "Roman numeral label IV in dark blue near the lower right endpoint",
    "movable candidate line with arrowheads at both ends, drawn as a bold dashed black or purple overlay",
    "highlight ring or glow around a clicked labeled line",
    "rise-over-run triangle markers on the selected line",
    "small text annotation showing selected line's slope as rise/run",
    "small text annotation showing selected line's y-intercept"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "slopeM",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Slope m",
      "range": [
        -4,
        4,
        0.1
      ],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "Changing m shows that positive slopes rise left to right, negative slopes fall left to right, and larger absolute values make the line visually steeper"
    },
    {
      "id": "interceptB",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "y-intercept b",
      "range": [
        -5,
        5,
        0.1
      ],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Changing b shows that the whole line shifts up or down while keeping the same slope, and that b is read where the line crosses the y-axis"
    },
    {
      "id": "selectLine",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Selecting one of lines I-IV isolates its intercept and rise-over-run so the learner can connect the visual graph to the matching equation"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start with the y-intercept",
      "narration": "Set b = 3 and notice that lines I, II, and IV all cross the y-axis at the same point. The y-intercept narrows the choices, but it does not identify the slope by itself.",
      "state": {
        "interceptB": 3,
        "slopeM": 0.5
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Use slope to separate shared intercepts",
      "narration": "With b fixed at 3, changing m rotates the candidate line around the point (0, 3). A shallow positive slope matches II, a steep positive slope matches I, and a steep negative slope matches IV.",
      "state": {
        "interceptB": 3,
        "slopeM": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same slope, different intercept",
      "narration": "Keep the steep positive slope and lower b from 3 to -3. The candidate line shifts down to match III, showing that parallel lines can have the same slope but different y-intercepts.",
      "state": {
        "interceptB": -3,
        "slopeM": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Click to read rise over run",
      "narration": "Click any labeled line to display its rise-over-run triangle. The triangle turns the line's steepness into the numerical slope used in y = mx + b.",
      "state": {
        "interceptB": 3,
        "slopeM": -2
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "To match a linear equation to a graph, first find where the line crosses the y-axis for b, then use the line's signed rise-over-run for m.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the original plot as a clean coordinate grid from about x = -7 to 7 and y = -5 to 5 with light gray unit squares and darker black axes through the origin. Draw four colored double-arrowed lines: I orange, steep positive, approximately y = 2x + 3; II light blue, shallow positive, approximately y = 0.5x + 3; III teal, steep positive, approximately y = 2x - 3; IV dark blue, steep negative, approximately y = -2x + 3. Place the Roman numeral labels near the same line ends as in the source image. Overlay the learner's candidate line as a dashed contrasting line controlled by m and b. When a line is clicked, dim the other lines slightly and draw a small right-triangle rise/run marker along the selected line, using upward rise for positive slopes and downward rise for negative slopes."
}

